icator with barCount = 5.', 'description': 'Validate the correct highest calculation over a normal window size that equals the series length. The highest at each index must be the maximum of the last up‑to‑5 values.'}, {'input': [{'parameter': 'barCount', 'value': 10}], 'expected': 'Using the same series [1, 3, 2, 5, 4] the returned NumericIndicator yields the same values as the previous case (1, 3, 3, 5, 5) because the window exceeds the series length; the indicator should treat missing earlier bars as unavailable and compute the maximum of all existing bars up to the current index.', 'description': 'Check behaviour when barCount is larger than the number of bars in the series – the highest indicator must gracefully fallback to the maximum of the available bars.'}, {'input': [{'parameter': 'barCount', 'value': 3}], 'expected': 'The original NumericIndicator remains unchanged: after calling highest(3) the original indicator still returns its original values (e.g., for the series [1,3,2,5,4] getValue(3) = 5). The newly created NumericIndicator returns the highest values (as in case 2). No side‑effects on the source indicator.', 'description': 'Ensure that highest(int) is non‑mutating; the source NumericIndicator must not be altered by the method call.'}, {'input': [{'parameter': 'barCount', 'value': 4}], 'expected': 'The delegate of the returned NumericIndicator is an instance of HighestValueIndicator and its internal barCount field equals 4 (verified via instanceof and reflection or by checking behaviour).', 'description': 'Confirm that the method wraps the source indicator with a HighestValueIndicator configured with the exact barCount supplied.'}]}]
